    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p28-p1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $f(x)$ cannot have any stationary points.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There are no stationary points.\n\nThe numerator of $f'(x)$ is a nonzero constant, and the denominator is positive throughout the domain.",
          "worked": "On the domain, $cx+d\\ne 0$. By the quotient rule,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{a(cx+d)-c(ax+b)}{(cx+d)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{ad-bc}{(cx+d)^2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nIf $c\\ne 0$ and the determinant is zero,\n\n$ad-bc=0,$\n\nthen $ax+b=\\frac{a}{c}(cx+d)$, making $f(x)=\\frac{a}{c}$ constant. If $c=0$, then $d\\ne 0$, so $ad-bc=0$ forces $a=0$, making $f(x)=\\frac{b}{d}$ constant. Since $f$ is nonconstant, $ad-bc\\ne 0$. Thus $f'(x)$ is never zero on the domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-p1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that if $f(x)$ is increasing, then $ad-bc>0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$ad-bc>0$, since a negative numerator would make $f$ decreasing on every interval in its domain.",
          "worked": "The denominator of $f'(x)$ is positive throughout the domain, so the derivative has the sign of $ad-bc$. Part (a) shows $ad-bc\\ne 0$. If $ad-bc<0$, then $f'<0$ throughout each interval of the domain and $f$ is decreasing there. Hence increasing behaviour requires $ad-bc>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 13
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-p1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "State what happens to $f(x)$ when $ad-bc<0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f$ is decreasing on each interval of its domain.",
          "worked": "When $ad-bc<0$, the numerator of $f'(x)$ is negative and the denominator is positive. Thus $f'(x)<0$ wherever $f$ is defined, so $f$ is decreasing on each interval of its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      }
    ]
  },
